{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1117 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "3167e019e3b67c52af67d591b9bbeb7127068360a85b98f0c64ccea2a6c05881" :edb/db-before-t 1116 :edb/db-after-t 1117 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534429 50 #inst "2026-10-07T03:03:54.986Z" 13194139534429 true] [17592186068427 1100 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2-2x + 3}\\, \\textrm{d}x$." 13194139534429 false] [17592186068427 1100 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2-2x + 3}\\, \\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139534429 true] [17592186068427 1145 "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x^2-2x + 3 & = x^2 - 2 \\cdot x+ (1^2 - 1^2) + 3 \\\\[3pt] & = (x^2 - 2 \\cdot x+ 1^2) - 1 + 3 \\\\[3pt] & = (x-1)^2+2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{1}{x^{2} - 2x + 3}dx &= ∫(1)/((x - 1)^{2} + 2)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫(1)/(1 + ((x - 1)/\\sqrt{2})^{2})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+u^2} \\, \\textrm{d}u = \\arctan u + C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\n$$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = \\sqrt{2}\\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u$, and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{1}{x^{2} - 2x + 3}dx &= \\frac{1}{2}∫(1)/(1 + ((x - 1)/\\sqrt{2})^{2})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫\\frac{1}{1 + u^{2}} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}∫\\frac{1}{1 + u^{2}}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\arctan u + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\arctan (\\frac{x - 1}{\\sqrt{2}}) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534429 false] [17592186068427 1145 "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nx^2-2x + 3\n& = x^2 - 2 \\cdot x+ (1^2 - 1^2) + 3 \\[3pt]\n& = (x^2 - 2 \\cdot x+ 1^2) - 1 + 3 \\[3pt]\n& = (x-1)^2+2\n}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-1)/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+u^2} \\, \\textrm{d}u\n=\n\\arctan u + C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = \\sqrt{2}\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-1)/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534429 true] [17592186068428 1100 "Calculate ${\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{4x - 4x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x}$." 13194139534429 false] [17592186068428 1100 "Calculate ${\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{4x - 4x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x}.$" 13194139534429 true] [17592186068428 1145 "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 4x^{2} &= - (4x^{2} - 4x) \\\\\n&= - ((2x)^{2} - 2 \\cdot 2x + 1 - 1) \\\\\n&= - ((2x - 1)^{2} - 1) \\\\\n&= 1 - (2x - 1)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4x - 4x^{2}}}dx &= ∫(1)/(\\sqrt{1 - (2x - 1)^{2}})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(2x - 1)$.\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$u =2x - 1$.\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac12\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u$, and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4x - 4x^{2}}}dx &= ∫(1)/(\\sqrt{1 - (2x - 1)^{2}})dx \\\\\n&= ∫\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\arcsin u + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\arcsin (2x - 1) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534429 false] [17592186068428 1145 "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-4{x}^{2} & =-(4{x}^{2}-4x) \\\\  & =-((2x)^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+1-1) \\\\  & =-((2x-1)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-(2x-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4x-4{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x-1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(2x - 1).$  \n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu =2x - 1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=2\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac12\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4x-4{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x-1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534429 true] [17592186068429 1100 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3} \\dfrac{1}{\\mid x-1 \\mid \\sqrt{x^2-2x}}\\, \\textrm{d}x}$." 13194139534429 false] [17592186068429 1100 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3} \\dfrac{1}{|x-1|\\sqrt{x^2-2x}}\\, \\textrm{d}x}.$" 13194139534429 true] [17592186068429 1145 "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x^2-2x&= x^2 -2x +(1-1) \\\\[3pt] &= (x^2 -2x +1)-1 \\\\[3pt] &= (x-1)^2-1 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3} \\dfrac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}x-1 & \\sqrt{x^2-2x}}\\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3} \\dfrac{1}{\\mid x-1\\end{vmatrix}\\sqrt{(x-1)^2-1}}\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\mid u \\mid \\sqrt{u^2-1}} \\, \\textrm{d}u = \\textrm{arcsec} \\, u +C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $x-1$.\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n$u=x-1$.\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u$.\n\n| $x$ | $1+\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3} \\dfrac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}x-1 & \\sqrt{x^2-2x}}\\, \\textrm{d}x &= \\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3}\\dfrac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}x-1 & \\sqrt{(x-1)^2-1}}\\,\\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &=\\int_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2} \\dfrac{1}{\\mid u\\end{vmatrix}\\sqrt{u^2-1}}\\, \\textrm{d}u \\\\[5pt] &= \\textrm{arcsec}\\, {u}\\Big\\end{vmatrix}_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\textrm{arcsec} \\left(2\\right) - \\textrm{arcsec}\\left(\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\right) \\\\[5pt] &=\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534429 false] [17592186068429 1145 "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    x^2-2x&= x^2 -2x +(1-1)\\[3pt]\n              &= (x^2 -2x +1)-1\\[3pt]\n         &= (x-1)^2-1\n         \n  }\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3} \\dfrac{1}{|x-1|\\sqrt{x^2-2x}}\\, \\textrm{d}x \n= \\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3} \\dfrac{1}{|x-1|\\sqrt{(x-1)^2-1}}\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^2-1}} \\, \\textrm{d}u = \\textrm{arcsec} \\, u +C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $x-1.$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x-1.\n$$\n\n \nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $1+\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3} \\dfrac{1}{|x-1|\\sqrt{x^2-2x}}\\, \\textrm{d}x \n&=  \\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3}\\dfrac{1}{|x-1|\\sqrt{(x-1)^2-1}}\\,\\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]\n&=\\int_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2} \\dfrac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^2-1}}\\, \\textrm{d}u\\[5pt]\n&=  \\textrm{arcsec}\\, {u}\\Big|_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\[5pt]\n&= \\textrm{arcsec} \\left(2\\right)  - \\textrm{arcsec}\\left(\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\right)\\[5pt]\n&=\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.\n\n}\n$$" 13194139534429 true] [17592186068430 1100 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{36 -(3x - 2)^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x$." 1