17592186068949 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534429 true] [17592186068949 1100 "$\\int \\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139534429 true] [17592186068949 1100 "What is $∫(1)/((2x - 3)^{2} + 4)dx$?" 13194139534429 false] [17592186068949 1145 "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{4((2x-3)^{2}/4+1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(2x-3)^{2}/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((2x-3)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{2x-3}{2}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2x-3}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((2x-3)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2x-3}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534429 true] [17592186069094 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534429 true] [17592186069094 1100 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-12x-35}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139534429 true] [17592186069094 1100 "Evaluate the integral $∫_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-x^{2} - 12x - 35}}dx$." 13194139534429 false] [17592186069094 1145 "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-12x-35 & =-({x}^{2}+12x+35) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+[{6}^{2}-{6}^{2}]+35) \\\\  & =-((x+6)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-(x+6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitutin